{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.5,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_rating",
      "components": ["T6", "D3"],
      "pins": ["T6.1", "T6.2", "T6.3"],
      "nets": ["ATO_VALVE (NET0035)", "+24V"],
      "obs": "The ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch is implemented with T6 = AO3400A, a 30 V N-channel MOSFET, switching a 24 V inductive load (V2 DIGITEN-24V) clamped only by D3 = SS34 (40 V). The intended 60 V AON6407 device is absent from the drawing (the only AON6407-equivalent part drawn is the P-channel ADIN6407 used for reverse-polarity protection).",
      "expected": "Spec §2 states the ATO valve is driven by 'T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET)', a 60 V device, chosen for the 24 V solenoid. A 30 V FET on a 24 V inductive rail has no headroom for switching/TVS transients (the 24 V rail is clamped at 28 V by the input TVS), so the part can avalanche and fail.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "ATO Valve block: T6 'AO3400A' low-side switch, D3 'SS34' flyback, load on '+24V' / V2 'DIGITEN-24V' (page 1, section E)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 — AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) | ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch"}
      ],
      "impact": "The ATO valve MOSFET is under-rated for the 24 V inductive load; repeated switching transients can exceed its 30 V Vds absolute maximum, leading to premature failure / loss of ATO control.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U5", "U9", "U4", "U10", "U8", "M5A"],
      "pins": ["U5.8", "U9.8", "U4.14", "U10.14", "U8.14", "M5A.IO6"],
      "nets": ["NET0048"],
      "obs": "The single net NET0048 ties together the PDIS (power-disable) pin of both ADM3260 isolators (U5 pin 8, U9 pin 8), the shared single-wire PD/UART pin of all three TMC2209 drivers (U4/U10/U8 pin 14), and ESP32 pin IO6 (J3.11, via R45).",
      "expected": "Spec keeps these as two separate nets: EZO_PDIS on IO6 ('ties both U3 pin 8 and U4 pin 8 ... to a single ESP32 pin (IO6/SPI_CLK)') and the shared driver UART bus STEP_BUS on IO21/IO22. Merging the isolator PDIS inputs onto the driver UART line means any UART traffic (or driver pull) toggles the isolators' power-disable, glitching the pH/EC I2C bridges, and the PDIS inputs load the UART bus.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "NET0048 label on U5 pin 8 (PDIS) and U9 pin 8 (PDIS); NET0048 on U4/U10/U8 pin 14 (PD/UART); NET0048 on M5A J3.11 = IO6/SPI_CLK with R45 1k"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "An EZO_PDIS net ties both U3 pin 8 and U4 pin 8 (PDIS = power-disable) to a single ESP32 pin (IO6/SPI_CLK) ... STEP_BUS (shared UART) | IO21/IO22"}
      ],
      "impact": "pH/EC isolated I2C bridges are power-cycled whenever the stepper drivers communicate on the UART line, and the UART bus is loaded by the PDIS inputs; the two control functions intended to be independent are shorted together.",
      "confidence": 0.4
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended design in two ways: the ATO solenoid is switched by a 30 V AO3400A instead of the specified 60 V AON6407 (under-rated for the 24 V inductive load), and the isolator PDIS line has been merged with the shared TMC2209 single-wire UART bus onto one net (NET0048/IO6), which the specification keeps separate."
}

